## Part 1: Introduction to components

Today we're learning to build what web developers call "components." It's the vague term nerds settled on to describe reusable pieces of code that, combined together, can create user interfaces on the web. A headline. A button. A chart. An image with a caption. You define it once and then use it everywhere.

Many of today's web frameworks are organized using components so that individual pieces of a page can be built in isolation, then assembled together without all of the code tangled up.

We see an example of this approach in our class template. Each component arranged in [`src/routes/+page.svelte`](https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template/blob/main/src/routes/%2Bpage.svelte) is imported from a separate file that defines its own structure, style and behavior. When you put them together, they create the page.

```svelte
<ArticleHeader
  {headline}
  {byline}
  {pubDate}
/>

<Image
  src="/example-photo.gif"
  alt="The Craig Newmark Graduate School of Journalism is at 219 West 40th Street in Midtown Manhattan."
  caption="The Craig Newmark Graduate School of Journalism is at 219 West 40th Street in Midtown Manhattan."
  credit="Craig Newmark Graduate School of Journalism"
/>
```

Our template uses a framework known as [SvelteKit](https://kit.svelte.dev/), which is built on top of the [Svelte](https://svelte.dev/) component framework. SvelteKit provides the overall structure for our project, while Svelte gives us the tools to build individual components.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-3/svelte-home.png"
  alt="Svelte homepage hero and navigation"
  chromeTitle="Svelte"
  chromeUrl="https://svelte.dev/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

[Svelte](https://svelte.dev/) was created in 2016 by [Rich Harris](https://rich-harris.dev) while he was living in Brooklyn and working as a graphics editor at The Guardian. His goal was to invent a better way to build interesting pages.

"Newsrooms are a very deadline-driven culture for obvious reasons, and so you need to build things quickly," Harris [told an interviewer in 2025](https://zencastr.com/z/nGDNQwB9). "Svelte was my attempt to make that easier."

He later moved to The New York Times, where he continued to develop Svelte as a tool for [building interactive news stories](https://www.nytimes.com/by/rich-harris). Today, it is used by newsrooms around the world, including The Guardian, The Times, Reuters, The Pudding and many more.

Svelte is also widely used outside of journalism, in companies like Apple, Square and Spotify. Harris now works on Svelte full-time at the technology company [Vercel](https://vercel.com/blog/vercel-welcomes-rich-harris-creator-of-svelte), which hosts many Svelte projects.

