You'll see three sections:

1. A `<script>` block at the top with snippets of JavaScript and what look like inputs
2. HTML markup in the middle with some braces and tags you might not recognize
3. A `<style>` block at the bottom filled with CSS

This three-part structure is the anatomy of every Svelte component. The script handles logic and data. The markup defines the structure. The styles control the appearance.

Seems simple enough, right?

Let's find out by building our own.

## Creating your first component

We'll start simple. We will build a component that displays a quote in large type, along with the name of the person being quoted.

Before we start coding, we should remember to install the project dependencies. Since we created a new repository for today's work, we'll need to do this step over again.

Open a terminal in Visual Studio Code and run the same command as last week. If you don't have it memorized yet, you will soon because you'll need to execute it every time you start a new project.

```bash
npm install
```

Then start the development server at [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173):

```bash
npm run dev
```

Now we're ready.

Right-click on the `src/lib/components/` folder in Visual Studio Code's left-hand Explorer and select "New File." Name it `Blockquote.svelte`.

The file will open in the editor, completely empty.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-3/vscode-blockquote-empty.png"
  alt="VS Code showing an empty Blockquote.svelte file"
  showChrome={false}
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

We'll build this component step by step, starting with the simplest possible version.

Begin by adding a traditional [`<blockquote>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/blockquote) tag to the file, just as you would in a traditional HTML document. Inside it, add a placeholder quote in the [`<p>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/p) tag and a [`<cite>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/cite) tag for the attribution.

```svelte
<blockquote>
  <p>
     As I look back over a misspent life
     I find myself more and more convinced
     that I had more fun doing news reporting
     than in any other enterprise.
     It is really the life of kings.
  </p>
  <cite>— H.L. Mencken</cite>
</blockquote>
```

This is HTML that you could find in any web page. If we included it in our page, it would render perfectly. But it's not reusable.

If we wanted to display another quote, we'd have to copy this snippet, paste it somewhere else and then change the content, which would duplicate work and open the door to errors and inconsistencies.

We can make our component reusable by replacing the hardcoded quote with a placeholder that accepts input from the outside. This is done using a Svelte feature called [`$props`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/$props), which is short for properties. It's how components receive data from the pages where they are put to use.

We can introduce properties to our blockquote by adding a `<script>` block at the top of the file. We can define variables for as many inputs as we like. They'll be passed in as a JavaScript object via Svelte's magic `$props()` function, which can be unpacked into separate variables using a technique called [destructuring](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Destructuring).

```svelte
<script>
  let { quote, attribution } = $props();
</script>
```

In this example, I've decided to allow two inputs: `quote` and `attribution`, which correspond to the content of the `<q>` and `<cite>` tags, respectively. You can name these variables whatever you like. They are just placeholders for whatever data you'd like to have passed in.

Next, we need to tell Svelte to insert these variables into the markup. We do this by replacing the hardcoded text with curly braces `{}` that reference our variables:

```svelte
<script>
  let { quote, attribution } = $props();
</script>

<blockquote>
  <p>{quote}</p>
  <cite>— {attribution}</cite>
</blockquote>
```

Before we test it out, let's make it look a little nicer.

Add a `<style>` tag at the bottom of the file, where we can write CSS. Here's something to get us started. Copy and paste what I've provided. It will use the color scheme defined in our project's `app.css` file, which is available to all components.

```svelte
<script>
  let { quote, attribution } = $props();
</script>

<blockquote>
  <p>{quote}</p>
  <cite>— {attribution}</cite>
</blockquote>

<style>
  blockquote {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    background-color: var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  p {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  cite {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    display: block;
    text-align: right;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
  }
</style>
```

These styles will only apply to the blockquote component, and won't affect any other blockquotes or paragraphs on the page. This is a technique that computer programmers refer to as [scoping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scope_(computer_programming)).

By limiting styles to a specific component, we can be confident that our CSS will not have unintended consequences elsewhere in our project. It's another way that components help us write clean, maintainable code.

Save your `Blockquote.svelte` file and we're ready to see it in action.

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` in your editor.

At the top of the file, add an import statement inside the existing `<script>` block, which will bring our new component into this page.

```js
import Blockquote from '$lib/components/Blockquote.svelte';
```

The [`$lib`](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/$lib) path is a special alias in SvelteKit projects that points to your `src/lib` folder. It saves you from writing long relative paths like `../../lib/components/`.

Now scroll down to the main content area of the page, inside of the `ArticleBody` component. This is where the content of our page lives. We can add our blockquote here between the existing paragraph tags. Feel free to put in any quote you like, or use the one I provided below.

```svelte
<Blockquote
  quote="As I look back over a misspent life I find myself more and more convinced that I had more fun doing news reporting than in any other enterprise. It is really the life of kings."
  attribution="H.L. Mencken"
/>
```

Save the file and check your browser at [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173). You should see a styled quote box with a left border and gray background.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-3/blockquote-page.png"
  alt="Local development page centered on the blockquote component"
  chromeTitle="localhost:5173"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Now add a second Blockquote with different text.

```svelte
<Blockquote
  quote="Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof; or abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press; or the right of the people peaceably to assemble, and to petition the Government for a redress of grievances."
  attribution="Constitution of the United States"
/>
```

Save the file and check your browser again. You should see two blockquotes, each with different content but identical styling.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-3/blockquote-both.png"
  alt="Local development page showing two blockquotes"
  chromeTitle="localhost:5174"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5174"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That's the power of components. You define it once and then use it everywhere. If you want to change the design, you only have to change it in one place, and all instances of that component will update automatically.

Take five to ten minutes to customize your blockquote. Try changing the border color, the background, the font size. Each change you make will update every blockquote on the page.

Try asking Copilot for edits.

Open the chat panel and ask it to "Make the blockquote font bigger" or "Make the blockquote background a light blue" or "Make the blockquote border dashed instead of solid."

Then try something broader like, "Make this blockquote component look like a 19th century newspaper."

You will see how the AI assistant is able to understand the structure of your component and make changes big and small. This is one of the most exciting aspects of coding with AI. These new tools can help you quickly iterate and explore variations without having to write every line of code yourself.

## Creating a parent component

Data journalism often needs to highlight key statistics. "500,000 people displaced." "A 47% increase." "$2.3 billion in losses." These numbers deserve special treatment.

Let's build a component that makes statistics stand out.

### Create the component file

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/BigNumber.svelte`. Start with this code:

```svelte
<script>
  let { number, label, context } = $props();
</script>

<div class="big-number">
  <span class="number">{number}</span>
  <span class="label">{label}</span>
  {#if context}
    <span class="context">{context}</span>
  {/if}
</div>

<style>
  .big-number {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background-color: #1a1a1a;
    color: white;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .number {
    display: block;
    font-size: 3rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    color: #f47920;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .context {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: #999;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
```

### Understanding optional props

Notice the `{#if context}` block in the markup. This is Svelte's conditional rendering syntax. It means "only render this part if `context` has a value."

This makes `context` an optional prop. You can use the component with or without it:

```svelte
<!-- With context -->
<BigNumber
  number="47%"
  label="Increase in ridership"
  context="Compared to pre-pandemic levels"
/>

<!-- Without context -->
<BigNumber
  number="$2.3B"
  label="Total investment"
/>
```

Both are valid. The first shows three lines, the second shows two. The component adapts.

### Add it to your page

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` again. Add another import at the top:

```js
import BigNumber from '$lib/components/BigNumber.svelte';
```

Then add a few big numbers to your page:

```svelte
<BigNumber
  number="1.2M"
  label="Students enrolled"
  context="Across all CUNY campuses"
/>

<BigNumber
  number="25"
  label="Colleges and schools"
/>

<BigNumber
  number="$7,680"
  label="Annual in-state tuition"
  context="For full-time undergraduates"
/>
```

Save and check your browser. You should see three dark boxes with orange numbers, stacked vertically.

They look good, but they'd look better side by side. That's what we'll tackle next.

## Part 4: Build a Row Component

So far, our components have been about displaying content. But components can also be about layout. Let's build a component that arranges other components in a horizontal row.

### Understanding children

Some components wrap other components. A navigation bar wraps menu items. A card grid wraps cards. A row wraps... whatever you put in it.

In Svelte 5, wrapped content is called "children." When you write:

```svelte
<Row>
  <BigNumber number="100" label="First" />
  <BigNumber number="200" label="Second" />
</Row>
```

The two BigNumber components are the "children" of the Row component. Row needs a way to receive and render them.

### Create the Row component

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/Row.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { children, gap = '1rem' } = $props();
</script>

<div class="row" style="gap: {gap};">
  {@render children()}
</div>

<style>
  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .row > :global(*) {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 200px;
    max-width: 300px;
  }
</style>
```

Let's break this down:

**`let { children, gap = '1rem' } = $props();`** - We're accepting two props. `children` is a special prop that contains whatever is placed between the opening and closing tags. `gap` has a default value of `'1rem'`, making it optional.

**`{@render children()}`** - This is how Svelte 5 renders children. It's a function call that outputs whatever was passed in.

**`style="gap: {gap};"`** - We're using the `gap` prop to set the CSS gap property inline. This lets users customize spacing.

**`:global(*)`** - The `:global()` modifier tells Svelte to apply styles to child elements even though they're technically outside this component's scope. Without it, the flex styling wouldn't reach the BigNumber components inside.

### Use the Row component

Back in `src/routes/+page.svelte`, add the import:

```js
import Row from '$lib/components/Row.svelte';
```

Now wrap your BigNumber components in a Row:

```svelte
<Row>
  <BigNumber
    number="1.2M"
    label="Students enrolled"
    context="Across all CUNY campuses"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number="25"
    label="Colleges and schools"
  />
  <BigNumber
    number="$7,680"
    label="Annual in-state tuition"
    context="For full-time undergraduates"
  />
</Row>
```

Save and check your browser. The three stat boxes should now sit side by side in a neat row.

Try resizing your browser window. Notice how the boxes wrap to a new line when the window gets too narrow. That's the `flex-wrap: wrap` doing its job, making our row responsive without any extra code.

### Customize the gap

The Row component accepts a `gap` prop. Try it:

```svelte
<Row gap="2rem">
  <!-- BigNumber components here -->
</Row>
```

The spacing between items increases. This kind of customization makes components flexible enough to use in different contexts.

### Nesting and composition

This pattern of components containing other components is called composition. It's one of the most powerful ideas in component-based development.

You can nest components as deep as you want. A Page might contain Sections. A Section might contain Rows. A Row might contain Cards. Each level handles one concern, making the code easier to understand and modify.

This is how real newsroom projects are built. The Reuters graphics kit, The Pudding's templates, the New York Times' interactives—they're all composed of small, focused components that snap together like building blocks.

## Homework Assignments

### Task 1: Build three original components

Invent and build three components that could be useful in a news story. They should be different from the ones we built in class.

Here are some ideas to get you started:

- **An author bio box** with photo, name, title, and social links
 **A "key takeaways" box** that lists bullet points in a highlighted container-
 **A "read more" card** that links out to another story with a thumbnail and headline
- **A correction or update box** for editor's notes
- **A data source attribution** that credits where numbers came from
-
You're not limited to these ideas. Be creative. Think about elements you see in news stories that could benefit from consistent styling.

### Task 2: Document your components

For each component you build, write a brief explanation in your project's README.md:

- What does the component do?
- What props does it accept?
- Why did you make the design choices you did?

This documentation practice is valuable. Real component libraries include documentation so other developers know how to use them.

### Task 3: Create a showcase page

Use your three new components on a page alongside the Blockquote, BigNumber, and Row components we built in class. The page should demonstrate all six components working together.

It doesn't need to be a real news story. It can be placeholder content. The goal is to show the components in context.

Deploy your page to GitHub Pages and send me the link before next Monday's class.

### Task 4: Prepare to present

Pick one of your original components to present in class next week. Be prepared to:

- Show the component on your live page
- Walk through the code
- Explain why you designed it the way you did
- Discuss any challenges you encountered
